2.2. System Control
If considering the linearized model in equations from (20) to (25) in the Laplace variable domain formulation, it is straightforward that the system is unstable due to the presence of poles in the origin. Additionally, the inputs U2, U3 and U4 directly affect the quadrotor’s attitude, while U1 directly affects the altitude dynamics. Figure 3 shows the proposed control system’s architecture, decoupled in two parts: the inner loop performs attitude stabilization, while the outer loop is responsible for altitude control and reference attitude computation, given a 3D trajectory to be followed. Each control input signal Ui is saturated before being given to the plant, i.e., the quadcopter. The lower and upper saturation bounds are UiLB and UiUB, respectively. The propellers’ capacity is assumed
to be such that the absolute value of the saturated total lift force U1SAT is equal to 3 mg.
Analogously, an absolute value of 1.5 mg refers to U2SAT and U3SAT, while an absolute value
equal to mgL refers to U4SAT.


